{
  "teamId": 141,
  "year": 2017,
  "narrative": "The 2017 Toronto Blue Jays arrived at spring training carrying the weight of expectations built on back-to-back playoff runs, only to stumble badly out of the gate. April was a disaster, the team going 8-17 as the bullpen — particularly Jason Grilli and Roberto Osuna — betrayed them at every turn. Mark Trumbo's opening-week homer off Grilli set an ugly tone, and a brutal late-April collapse against St. Louis, when Randal Grichuk went deep off Osuna to erase a lead, underscored just how fragile this roster had become. The one saving grace was Kendrys Morales, who strung together a series of clutch home runs to keep the team from falling even further off the pace.\n\nMay was a revelation. The Blue Jays went 18-10, their best month of the season, riding a power surge from José Bautista and Morales while the lineup found a rhythm it had lacked all spring. Bautista launched a pair of three-run shots that energized Rogers Centre, and Kevin Pillar's go-ahead blast in mid-May provided the kind of walk-off electricity the team desperately needed. The bullpen still burned them at times — Danny Barnes surrendering a three-run homer to Welington Castillo twice in two days — but the offense was explosive enough to mask those wounds and carry Toronto back into contention.\n\nJune and July told a tale of promise deflated. The team hovered around .500 but could never break through, and Jason Grilli's catastrophic double allowed to Whit Merrifield in late June — a swing of nearly .77 win probability — was the single most damaging moment of the entire season. July brought a Steve Pearce grand slam that felt like a turning point, and Justin Smoak and Morales kept slugging, but Roberto Osuna's save situation collapses in back-to-back games against Chicago on July 31st effectively killed any realistic playoff hope. The closer who had been so reliable was suddenly a liability in the highest-leverage moments.\n\nAugust and September were a slow fade. Osuna continued to struggle in close games, allowing Alex Bregman's go-ahead triple and a game-breaking hit against the Cubs in August. The September series against Baltimore turned into another gut-punch as Osuna surrendered a Welington Castillo homer and Danny Barnes got tagged late. Justin Smoak's remarkable individual season — he slugged his 38th home run in September — provided one of the few bright spots in an otherwise painful finish. Toronto ended 76-86, a decade-low in wins, outscored by 91 runs, and left to reckon with a roster that had aged past its window of contention.",
